Starting a long skill run at your desk and checking on it from somewhere else is the obvious use of Claude Code Remote Control. For people who write and run skills, subagent fan-outs and multi-step SKILL.md workflows, the interesting questions are narrower than whether it connects. What does a phone actually show of a run that has subagents going in the background? Which prompts reach you, which ones stop the run until you answer, and which skills are reasonable to leave alone while you are away?

This article answers those questions from Anthropic's own documentation, checked on October 28, 2026. The documented requirements are short: a claude.ai login on a Pro, Max, Team or Enterprise plan (an Owner has to switch it on for Team and Enterprise), no API keys, and no Amazon Bedrock, Google Cloud or Microsoft Foundry routing. The full list is on the [Remote Control page](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/remote-control). Nothing below comes from our own testing. Where the docs state a behavior, we cite it. Where we offer a judgment, such as which skills to leave unattended, we label it as ours.

## What the phone sees of a skill run

Remote Control does not move the run anywhere. The session keeps executing on your machine, and the phone or browser is a window into it. Everything the session already has stays local: the project files, MCP servers, tools, hooks and the skills installed on that host. A skill does not need to exist on the phone, because the phone never executes anything.

The docs make one point that matters for subagent-heavy skills. The conversation and the progress of subagents and dynamic workflows stay in sync across the terminal, the browser and the phone. When a device connects, it shows any subagents and workflows the session already has running in the background, and stopping one of them from the device stops that task on your machine. So the stop button on a phone is a real control over a fan-out, not a view-only indicator.

What you can send is wider than plain messages. Photos you attach are seen by Claude directly. Other files are downloaded to the host and passed in as `@` references, and if a file cannot be downloaded, Claude receives a note such as `[1 of 3 attachments did not arrive]` in its place. Several built-in commands also work from mobile and web, and a few of them are directly useful while a long skill is running:

- `/context`, `/compact` and `/clear` for managing a long session.
- `/usage` to see where you stand against plan limits.
- `/model` and `/effort`, which take their value as an argument on mobile and web, for example `/model sonnet` or `/effort high`.
- `/rename`, which changes the session title that shows up in the list.
- `/recap`, `/mcp` (a text summary on mobile) and `/config key=value`.

Some commands are local-only. `/plugin` and `/resume` work only from the local CLI. The `/mcp` picker is not available on mobile or web, so a claude.ai connector that needs re-authorization has to be handled from the host with `claude mcp login "claude.ai Slack" --no-browser`, followed by `/mcp reconnect`. A line that starts with `!` sent from the phone goes to Claude as a message and does not run in shell mode.

The docs contain one sentence that is specifically about skills. They say `/claude-api` is unavailable when you type it from mobile or web, and that Claude can still load that skill on its own there. That is the only skill-related statement in the pages we read. Whether typing a custom skill's slash command from a phone works the same way is not documented, and we did not test it. Plan around Claude loading a skill itself from a plain-language request, and verify the typed form on your own setup before you rely on it.

{% figure src="https://academy.claude.com/og/tutorials/using-claude-code-remote-control.png" alt="Claude Academy title card for the tutorial Using Claude Code Remote Control, with a terminal mock-up showing a remote control active line and an awaiting approval on mobile line" caption="Title card of Anthropic's Academy tutorial on Remote Control. The terminal inside it is an illustration of the approve-from-your-phone flow, not a captured session. (Image: Claude Academy, Anthropic)" /%}

## The approval loop on a phone

Supervising a skill from a phone is mostly about approvals. Two documented behaviors shape how that loop feels.

### What reaches your phone

Both permission prompts and `AskUserQuestion` questions are forwarded to the connected device, and Claude Code leaves them open until someone answers. Notifications are what tell you a run is waiting. With Remote Control active, Claude may push a message to your phone, usually at the end of a long task or when a decision is needed. You can also ask for one in your prompt; the docs give "notify me when the tests finish" as an example. To set it up, install the app, sign in with the same account, grant the operating system's notification permission, then switch push on in `/config`, choosing proactive notifications, permission prompts and questions, or both. Finer per-event settings do not exist.

For skill authors the practical reading is that the two toggles decide whether a stalled run can reach you at all. A run that needs approval for a new tool but has the permission-prompt toggle off will sit until you open the app on your own.

### What waits and what expires

Not every dialog behaves the same way, and the differences decide whether a run is safe to leave.

| Item | Documented behavior |
|---|---|
| Permission prompt | Stays open until you answer it |
| `AskUserQuestion` question | Stays open until you answer it |
| Other forwarded dialogs, such as the model-choice prompt after a safety refusal | Closes after five minutes by default and takes its no-action default; `dialogExpiry` adjusts or disables it (v2.1.224 or later) |
| Usage-credits consent prompt for Fable models | Shown only where the session runs, not on your device; if nobody at the terminal answers before it closes, the turn ends without sending the request |
| claude.ai connector sign-in | The `/mcp` picker is unavailable on mobile and web; authorize from the host shell |

Two consequences follow. First, a run with many permission prompts becomes a series of waits, and the docs give no expiry for those prompts. A skill that asks for approval at step three of twelve will pause there for as long as you are out of reach. Second, a few dialogs do not wait at all. They expire into a default, or end a turn, so a run that depends on one of them is not as patient as the permission prompts are.

{% figure src="https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HEnnQaub0AAavjA.jpg" alt="iPhone screenshot of the Claude app Code tab showing a session titled Add Vim keybindings for same-line navigation in claude-cli-internal, with a Changes list naming src/vim/motions.ts and src/vim/transitions.ts" caption="Screenshot attached to the first post in Boris Cherny's thread about the Claude mobile app: a coding session summary with a list of changed files in the Code tab. It shows what a mobile review of a finished run can look like; it does not by itself prove the session was a Remote Control session. (Image: Boris Cherny of the Claude Code team, via @bcherny on X, March 30, 2026)" /%}

