## Permission settings that matter for unattended skills

### Modes, as offered to a Remote Control session

According to the permission-modes page, four modes can be picked from the app when you began the session yourself on your local machine: Manual, Accept edits, Plan and Auto. Bypass permissions cannot be chosen from the app. The switch is a dropdown below the prompt box on claude.ai/code; in the mobile app it is the plus button in the prompt box, followed by Permission. Whatever mode the local session is in, including one set in the terminal, is what the dropdown displays, and a change made on either side appears on the other. A starting mode can also be set when you launch the server, as in `claude remote-control --permission-mode acceptEdits`.

| Mode | What the docs say | Fit for a skill run (our judgment) |
|---|---|---|
| Manual | Prompts on first use of each tool | Every new kind of tool call pings the phone; good for the first run of an unfamiliar skill |
| Accept edits | Auto-accepts file edits and common filesystem commands such as `mkdir`, `touch`, `mv` and `cp` in the working directory or `additionalDirectories` | Fits skills that only change files in a worktree |
| Plan | Claude reads files and runs read-only shell commands but does not edit your source files | Fits research and review skills |
| Auto | A background classifier checks actions before they run; reduces prompts but does not guarantee safety; explicit ask rules still force a prompt | Fits longer runs, paired with ask rules for the steps you care about |
| Bypass permissions | Skips prompts; the docs advise isolated environments such as containers or VMs; not selectable from the app | Not a phone-supervision mode |
| dontAsk | Auto-denies anything that would prompt; described for locked-down CI and scripts; not in the app's list | A different tool, aimed at scripts rather than supervised runs |

Auto mode comes with availability conditions. As of Claude Code v2.1.283, terminal and VS Code interactive sessions start in it by default, and the docs list model and provider requirements. Its classifier also screens every message Claude sends to another agent through `SendMessage`, which is relevant when a skill drives an agent team. According to the docs, auto mode cuts down on permission prompts without guaranteeing safety, and it is no substitute for review of sensitive operations.

The docs also describe a no-classifier alternative for fewer prompts: Manual mode with the Bash sandbox in auto-allow mode, started with `claude --permission-mode default` and then `/sandbox`, on macOS, Linux and WSL2. For a Remote Control server specifically, the `--sandbox` and `--no-sandbox` flags were removed in v2.1.300, and sandboxing is turned on in a settings file instead.

### Rules, and where they are enforced

Permission rules are evaluated in a fixed order: deny, then ask, then allow. The first match decides, and specificity does not change that order, so an allow rule cannot carve an exception out of a deny rule. The docs also state that permission rules are enforced by Claude Code and not by the model. Instructions in your prompt or in `CLAUDE.md` shape what Claude tries to do, but they do not change what Claude Code allows.

That sentence has a direct consequence for skill authors, and the inference is ours. A line in a SKILL.md that says "never push without asking" is a request to the model. If a push really must reach your phone first, that needs an ask rule or a PreToolUse hook. Rules work whichever device you are using, so they are the right place for the steps you would otherwise try to protect with wording.

### Gating subagent spawns and skills by parameter

Deny and ask rules can match a top-level input parameter on built-in tools, written `Tool(param:value)`. The docs give three examples that matter for fan-out skills:

- `Agent(model:opus)` matches Agent calls that request the Opus model tier.
- `Agent(isolation:worktree)` matches Agent calls that request a git worktree.
- `Bash(run_in_background:true)` matches Bash calls that run in the background.

An ask rule on the first one turns every Opus-tier subagent spawn into a decision on your phone, which is a cost gate rather than a safety gate. The same page notes that a `Skill(skill:)` deny rule matches the skill under any of its names, such as its alias or display name, so you can remove a skill you never want running unattended. We have not tested these rules. The docs add several limits: the parameter has to be a direct field of the tool input, a parameter the model omits is never matched, the value is compared against the literal input Claude sends before any normalization, and `Agent(model:opus)` matches the alias `opus` but not a full model ID. They suggest running with `--verbose` to see the exact parameter names and values in each tool call.

An illustrative settings block, not a recommendation for your repository:

```json
{
  "permissions": {
    "deny": ["Bash(git push *)"],
    "ask": ["Agent(model:opus)", "Bash(run_in_background:true)"]
  }
}
```

Rules on command text have an inherent gap, because the same action can be written many ways. The docs point to sandboxing for filesystem and network enforcement that does not depend on the command text, and to a PreToolUse hook when you want your own logic to inspect the full command before it runs.

### Project skills and workspace trust

If you run skills that came from a repository you did not write, one row in the docs' trust table deserves a read. Hooks in settings files, the `env` block, helper commands, and a project skill's hooks and `allowed-tools` are all used before you trust the folder, and the docs add that workspace trust never gates a skill's `allowed-tools` in any session. By contrast, frontmatter hooks in a project subagent are not used until you accept trust. The documented default for a Remote Control server is also relevant: in an untrusted directory it asks `Trust? [y/N]`, and in a non-terminal context it exits with "Workspace not trusted". So an unattended server that exits on first start is behaving as documented, and the fix is to trust the directory once from a terminal.

The practical rule that follows, again our reading: open the SKILL.md frontmatter of any third-party skill before you let it run with nobody watching, and look at what it pre-approves.

## Which skills are safe to leave running

The documented behaviors above suggest four questions to ask of any skill before leaving it alone. Does it need an approval that will block? Do its effects stay inside the checkout or worktree? Can git undo them? Does anything leave the machine? The tiers below are an editorial framework built on those questions, not something Anthropic publishes.

| Tier | Typical skill work | Suggested settings | Leave it unattended? |
|---|---|---|---|
| A: read-only | Research, code review, planning, repository summaries | Plan mode, or Manual with narrow allow rules | Yes; wait for the completion push |
| B: edits in a worktree | Refactors, test generation, documentation updates on a branch | Accept edits with `--spawn worktree`; deny rules on push and deploy commands | Yes for a bounded task; review the diff on return |
| C: effects outside the checkout | Pushing, opening pull requests, deploying, sending messages, migrations | Manual or Auto with ask rules; approve each step from the phone | Only while you can answer; the session waits |
| D: irreversible or credentialed | Deleting data, production changes, rotating keys | Do not run from a phone | No |

Worktree isolation is what makes tier B workable. In server mode, `--spawn worktree` gives each on-demand session its own git worktree, so parallel sessions do not edit the same directory. It needs a git repository or a WorktreeCreate hook, and pressing `w` at runtime toggles between same-directory and worktree spawn, with the choice saved for the project. The default capacity is 32 concurrent sessions.

### Examples from the registry

We did not run any of the skills below. The classification comes from their registry descriptions, which means it is a starting point to confirm by reading each SKILL.md.

| Skill | Registry description | Likely tier, and why |
|---|---|---|
| [code-review](https://agentskills.codes/skills/code-review) | "Code review assistance with linting, style checking, and best practices" | A, or B if it applies fixes; reading and reporting are easy to leave alone |
| [code-change-verification](https://agentskills.codes/skills/code-change-verification) | Runs the mandatory verification stack when changes affect runtime code, tests or build behavior in the OpenAI Agents Python repository | A or B; it is tied to that repository, so it is a pattern to copy rather than a drop-in |
| [create-worktree-skill](https://agentskills.codes/skills/create-worktree-skill) | A skill that creates a worktree when the user asks for one | B; git state changes locally and is reversible |
| [create-handoff](https://agentskills.codes/skills/create-handoff) | "Create handoff document for transferring work to another session" | A or B; it writes a document, and the document is a good artifact to read on a phone |
| [debug-hooks](https://agentskills.codes/skills/debug-hooks) | "Systematic hook debugging workflow" | B; hooks execute commands, so changes to them deserve a review before the next run |
| [create-subagents](https://agentskills.codes/skills/create-subagents) | Guidance for creating and using Claude Code subagents and the Task tool | A for the skill itself; the risk sits in the subagents it helps you define, which should get the narrowest tools |

For model and effort choices across a fan-out, our earlier piece on [Opus 5.5 vs Sonnet 5.5 for agent skills](/blog/opus-5-5-vs-sonnet-5-5-for-agent-skills) covers routing per skill. For a comparison of how another agent product frames approvals, see [OpenAI dots: the permission model and what to hand off](/blog/openai-dots-permissions-and-workflows).

## Setting up a host for supervised runs

The documented pieces combine into a short setup. The commands and flags below come from the Remote Control page; the combination is our arrangement.

```bash
# on the always-on host, inside tmux so an SSH drop does not end the session
tmux new -s skills
cd ~/projects/my-app
claude remote-control --name "my-app skills" --spawn worktree --permission-mode acceptEdits
```

The docs recommend tmux or screen on a remote machine so the session survives an SSH disconnect. A few settings are worth deciding before you leave:

1. Sign in with a claude.ai account, not an API key or a `claude setup-token` token. Tokens from `setup-token` and `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` only make model requests and cannot establish Remote Control sessions.
2. Name the server. `--name` sets the title in the session list, and with several sessions a legible name is how you find the right one on a small screen.
3. Turn on push in `/config`, including the permission-prompt and question toggle, and install the app on the same account.
4. Pick the starting permission mode on purpose with `--permission-mode`. An unrecognized mode stops the server at startup.
5. Trust the directory once from a terminal so an unattended start does not exit on the trust prompt.
6. Run `/mcp` checks and connector authorizations before you leave, because the picker is not available from the phone.

Auto-connecting sessions is possible with `remoteControlAtStartup`, but only your user settings can switch it on, because a true value in project or local settings is ignored. A global flag written before `remote-control` is not passed to the sessions the server creates. For some flags, such as `--settings`, Claude Code refuses to start and tells you which one to remove.

