## Writing SKILL.md files that supervise well

The documentation does not cover skill authoring for remote use, so this section is our suggestion, built on the documented behaviors and untested.

1. **Surface decisions as questions, not paragraphs.** `AskUserQuestion` questions stay open until answered, and the push toggle covers questions. A skill that needs a human choice and asks for it as a question reaches the phone as something you can answer in one tap. Whether a skill can reliably trigger that tool is something to check on your setup.
2. **Do not put enforcement in prose.** Rules are enforced by Claude Code, so a step that must pause for a person belongs in an ask rule or a hook, with the SKILL.md wording as a backup.
3. **End each phase with a short status line.** On a phone you read the tail of a session. A one-line summary after each phase makes a long run legible without scrolling.
4. **Write results to files in the worktree.** A report on disk survives a dropped connection and a resumed session, and it can be attached to a pull request later.
5. **Make steps safe to repeat.** The docs say a crashed server-mode session is served again when you send a message from a connected device (v2.1.238 or later), and `--continue` and `--session-id` restore sessions for about four hours after a server stops (v2.1.200 or later). A skill whose steps can be repeated without damage fits that model better than one that assumes a single clean pass.
6. **Ask for a notification at the end.** The docs' own example is a prompt line asking to be notified when the tests finish, and the same trick works for the last phase of a skill.
7. **Keep local-only commands out of the middle of a run.** If a skill expects `/plugin`, `/resume` or the `/mcp` picker mid-flow, a phone cannot supply them.

## What to check when you come back

A dropped connection looks the same whether the work stopped or kept going. The docs say that the "Remote Control disconnected" and "Can't reach" messages mean your browser or app lost contact with Claude Code on your computer, and neither message tells you whether Claude or a subagent you were watching is still working. The state to check is on the host, in the terminal where Claude Code runs.

| What happened | Documented behavior | What to do |
|---|---|---|
| Machine slept or network dropped (interactive session) | Reconnects on its own when the machine or network returns; retries as long as the outage lasts | Open the session list; the session should come back |
| Long outage (server mode) | Gives up after roughly 10 minutes and the process exits | Run `claude remote-control` again, then `-c` to continue |
| HTTP 403 refusals after a VPN or network change | Interactive sessions retry for up to three minutes, then disconnect and name what refused | Check the VPN, proxy and firewall |
| No contact with the server for about 30 minutes | An interactive session can disconnect with a message saying so | Run `/remote-control` to reconnect |
| Server-mode session crashed | Send a message from a connected device and Claude Code serves it again (v2.1.238 or later) | No restart needed |
| Server stopped | Sessions can be restored for about four hours with `--continue` or `--session-id` (v2.1.200 or later) | Resume inside that window, or start fresh |
| Approval is waiting | The prompt stays open until answered | Answer it, or lower what needs approval next time |

On return, check the worktree diff before anything else, then the subagent list, then `/context` and `/usage` to see how much of the session and your plan the run consumed. For a longer explanation of connection errors and what each documented message means, the Remote Control page's troubleshooting section lists a cause and fix for every message.

## What we could not verify

- We did not test Remote Control or any skill named here. Every behavior is as documented by Anthropic on October 28, 2026, and the docs cite many version numbers, so compare `claude --version` against them.
- Whether typing a custom skill's slash command from mobile or web works is not stated. The only skill-related statement we found is the `/claude-api` sentence.
- The docs do not say how a pending permission prompt in one subagent affects the others in a fan-out. We have described the prompt as waiting, which is what the docs say, and nothing beyond that.
- We could not confirm that a server started with `--permission-mode` accepts every mode listed in the permission-modes page. The documented example uses `acceptEdits`.
- The Remote Control page lists no usage quota specific to the feature. How a supervised run counts against your plan is not covered in the pages we read.
- The tier framework and the registry classifications are our judgment from documented behavior and registry descriptions. They are not tested results.
- Early write-ups describe a narrower feature than today's docs; for example, a February 2026 write-up by Simon Willison reported one session at a time. Treat anything dated before this page as history. The current default server capacity is 32 sessions.

## Verdict

Remote Control is a reasonable way to supervise skill and subagent work that already runs on a machine you control, because it keeps the local tools, hooks and skills in place and gives the phone real controls: answering prompts, stopping background tasks and switching modes. What it does not do is make an unattended run safe by itself. The safety comes from what you decide before you walk away: a starting mode, ask and deny rules for the steps that matter, a worktree per session, and a skill that is shaped to be read on a small screen.

If you take one thing from this article, take the split between prose and rules. Anything you need to stop before it happens belongs in a rule or a hook that Claude Code enforces. Anything you merely want to nudge belongs in the SKILL.md. Tier A and B skills fit supervision from a phone well. Tier C skills work while you can answer, and tier D skills should stay at a keyboard.

